  {"claim_ref": "book_0103::lead_book_0103_1_053", "verdict": "corroboration", "behavior_id": "trend_following_channel", "page": 1, "exact_text": "Another way to use the channel technique is to spot fail-\nures to reach the channel line, usually a sign of a weakening trend.", "added_conditions": ["the failure of any move within an established price channel to reach one side of the channel usually indicates that the trend is shifting, and increases the likelihood that the other side of the channel will be broken"], "added_parameters": []},
  {"claim_ref": "book_0103::lead_book_0103_1_055", "verdict": "corroboration", "behavior_id": "trend_continuation_pullback", "page": 1, "exact_text": "If the prior trend is to be main-\ntained, the correction must stop at the two-thirds point. This then\nbecomes a relatively low risk buying area in an uptrend or selling\narea in a downtrend.", "added_conditions": ["if prices move beyond the two-thirds point, the odds then favor a trend reversal rather than just a retracement"], "added_parameters": [{"name": "minimum retracement", "value": "33%", "page": 1}, {"name": "maximum retracement", "value": "66%", "page": 1}, {"name": "typical retracement", "value": "50%", "page": 1}]},
  {"claim_ref": "book_0103::lead_book_0103_1_056", "verdict": "corroboration", "behavior_id": "trend_continuation_pullback", "page": 1, "exact_text": "If an uptrend is in the process of correcting itself, the\ndownside correction will usually stop at the higher speedline (the\n2/, speedline). If not, prices will drop to the lower speedline (the\n1/, speedline).", "added_conditions": ["if the lower line is also broken, prices will probably continue all the way to the beginning of the prior trend", "only when the upper (2/3) line is broken would a signal be given that the old highs will probably be challenged"], "added_parameters": [{"name": "speedline support levels", "value": "1/3 and 2/3", "page": 1}]},
  {"claim_ref": "book_0103::lead_book_0103_1_059", "verdict": "corroboration", "behavior_id": "capitulation_exhaustion", "page": 1, "exact_text": "The chart action of October 28, 1997 was a classic exam-\nple of an upside reversal day or a \u201cselling climax.\u201d Prices opened sharply\nlower and closed sharply higher. The unusually heavy volume bar for that\nday added to its importance.", "added_conditions": ["an upside reversal day is more important when accompanied by unusually heavy volume"], "added_parameters": []},
  {"claim_ref": "book_0103::lead_book_0103_2_070", "verdict": "generic", "behavior_id": null, "page": null, "exact_text": null, "added_conditions": [], "added_parameters": []},
